-- Refine group composition and illustrated likeness for existing GTA VI styles.
update public.styles as style
set prompt = revised.prompt
from (values
  (
    '6e723982-219f-49aa-bc40-f9d562ea954c'::uuid,
    $style_prompt$Likeness is the highest priority. The people and pets in all the uploaded photos determine the cast size. Draw each distinct subject exactly once, using the clearest view of anyone who appears in more than one photo. Match each person’s facial proportions, including the shape and spacing of their eyes and brows, their eye color when visible, and the shape of their nose, mouth, and jawline. Retain their natural skin tone, apparent age, build, hairline, and hairstyle. Preserve visible differences in height and build when people are pictured together. Keep visible identifying details such as freckles, facial hair, glasses, or tattoos. Preserve each pet's face shape, coat colors, and markings. Keep every face unobstructed and distinguishable. Add no extra people or pets.

Create a GTA VI cover-art montage with roughly eight asymmetric comic panels as a starting point. Vary their sizes and angles, with thick near-black plum gutters and a matching narrow outer keyline. Frame a solo subject or a small group chest- or waist-up in the largest panel toward the upper right, in contemporary South Florida streetwear. For a larger cast, widen that panel, stagger faces at different heights, or divide the cast across several character panels so each face has room. Adjust the panel count and reduce the space for scenery as needed. Keep panel edges clear of faces, and never repeat a subject across panels. Fill the remaining panels with Vice City vignettes such as a blue helicopter over pastel towers, a hot-pink sport bike, a yellow supercar, a speedboat in turquoise water, or a palm-lined Art Deco street.

Draw the people as stylized comic-cover figures. Use selective dark contours, broad, flat color shapes, and angular shadows across cheeks, brows, and necks. Define eyes and asymmetries with a few purposeful lines and simplify hair into drawn locks. Faces should be recognizable through their shape and identifying details, with the same graphic treatment as the rest of the artwork. Leave out photographic skin texture and glossy 3D shading. Give each panel a limited palette, alternating turquoise and indigo with coral pink, tropical violet, and hot yellow-orange. Use warm lit edges and cool purple shadows while preserving each person's complexion.

Lay the logo across the middle of the montage and overlap the panel gutters where the faces leave space. Scale or shift the logo to keep it clear of every face. Stack the white lowercase words "grand", "theft", and "auto" on three close-set lines in heavy squared lettering with a thick dark outline. Behind them, place a large "VI" with a white keyline and a violet-to-fuchsia-to-golden-orange fill with faint palm silhouettes. Place a small yellow square with the black R and white star near the lower right. Use only the specified lettering.

Compose one full-bleed A-series portrait poster (width to height 1:√2). Keep faces and lettering clear of the trim while carrying the panel art and graphic edges all the way to the paper edges. Output the flat artwork alone, with no frame, mat, mockup, or watermark.$style_prompt$
  ),
  (
    '91e35d81-5227-4910-b055-681a91912cba'::uuid,
    $style_prompt$Likeness is the highest priority. The people and pets in all the uploaded photos determine the cast size. Draw each distinct subject exactly once, using the clearest view of anyone who appears in more than one photo. Match each person’s facial proportions, including the shape and spacing of their eyes and brows, their eye color when visible, and the shape of their nose, mouth, and jawline. Retain their natural skin tone, apparent age, build, hairline, and hairstyle. Preserve visible differences in height and build when people are pictured together. Keep visible identifying details such as freckles, facial hair, glasses, or tattoos. Preserve each pet's face shape, coat colors, and markings. Keep every face unobstructed and distinguishable. Add no extra people or pets.

Create a GTA VI character campaign poster as one continuous, cinematic Vice City scene. Frame a solo subject or a small group waist- or mid-thigh-up, with open sky above and a low horizon behind them. Use a slightly low viewpoint and poised, purposeful stances. For a larger cast, widen the camera view and arrange people across shallow, staggered rows, with face positions offset at different heights so no one is hidden behind a shoulder. Let the cast take up more of the frame and simplify background details when space is tight. Dress people in contemporary South Florida streetwear suited to their age and build. Use one setting such as a waterfront promenade, palm-lined boulevard, or rooftop overlooking the bay, with pastel Art Deco buildings or distant high-rises.

Draw the subjects as stylized, hand-painted game campaign characters. Preserve recognizable facial proportions and expressions with tapered linework around eyes and hair, sculpted planes of color on foreheads and cheekbones, crisp cast shadows, and a little drawn hatching. Render bodies and cloth with broad, drawn shapes while retaining each person’s natural proportions. Keep the illustrated finish consistent from faces to background, with no photographic skin texture or glossy 3D shading. Set the scene at golden hour or dusk with a lavender-to-peach sky, apricot rim light, and mauve and indigo shadows balanced by cool teal accents. Preserve individual complexions through the colored lighting.

Center the solid-white title in the open upper sky, stacking "grand", "theft", and "auto" on three close-set lines of heavy squared lowercase lettering. Place a separate thin white outline "VI" near the bottom center and a small yellow R-star square near the lower right. Use a more compact title when the cast needs extra space. Keep all lettering clear of faces and use only the specified title and badge.

Compose one full-bleed A-series portrait poster (width to height 1:√2). Keep faces and lettering clear of the trim while extending the continuous scene to all four edges. Output the flat artwork alone, with no frame, mat, mockup, or watermark.$style_prompt$
  ),
  (
    '6140eddf-6834-4fe2-b510-384db9b06706'::uuid,
    $style_prompt$Likeness is the highest priority. The people and pets in all the uploaded photos determine the cast size. Draw each distinct subject exactly once, using the clearest view of anyone who appears in more than one photo. Match each person’s facial proportions, including the shape and spacing of their eyes and brows, their eye color when visible, and the shape of their nose, mouth, and jawline. Retain their natural skin tone, apparent age, build, hairline, and hairstyle. Preserve visible differences in height and build when people are pictured together. Keep visible identifying details such as freckles, facial hair, glasses, or tattoos. Preserve each pet's face shape, coat colors, and markings. Keep every face unobstructed and distinguishable. Add no extra people or pets.

Create a GTA VI soundtrack sleeve as one continuous, quiet sunset scene. Arrange the cast in the lower portion beside a vintage muscle car seen broadside. For a solo subject or a small group, use relaxed poses leaning or resting against the car. For a larger cast, spread people along the car and nearby waterfront, using a mix of seated and standing poses at different heights. Widen the view and let the group take up more of the scene before shrinking faces or obscuring anyone. Keep the car as a horizontal element behind the group. Use simple casual clothes and contemplative expressions where the source pose allows. Keep the low waterfront skyline hazy with dark wind-bent palms and a few distant birds. Reserve open sky above the group for the album title.

Draw people as stylized soundtrack-cover illustration, using fine dark contours, simplified cheek and jaw planes, a few drawn marks in the hair and fabric, and broad violet-blue shadow shapes. Define recognizable eyes, expressions, and distinguishing features with line and shape. Use narrow peach and gold rim highlights and keep each person's skin undertone identifiable in the cooler shadows. Leave out photographic skin texture and glossy 3D shading. Reserve soft gradients for the slate-blue, lavender, dusty pink, and coral sky, the pale golden horizon, long car reflections, and a restrained neon glow. The mood is wistful and unhurried.

Set "GRAND THEFT AUTO" across the top in small, widely spaced white capitals. Beneath it, center a hollow "VI" drawn with a fine glowing white outline so the sky shows through. Overlay it with the words "The Album" in rising white brush script with a sweeping underline and gentle pink-white halo. Scale the title to the available sky above the faces. Place a small white outlined R-star badge near the lower right. Use only the specified lettering.

Compose one full-bleed A-series portrait poster (width to height 1:√2). Keep faces and lettering clear of the trim while extending the sky, car, and surroundings to the paper edges. Output the flat artwork alone, with no frame, mat, mockup, or watermark.$style_prompt$
  )
) as revised(id, prompt)
where style.id = revised.id;
